EXT. SANDBAGGED FIELD HOSPITAL - TUNISIA DESERT - DAY

A tent village of bleached canvas and red-cross banners. Dust. Generators. A string of ambulances nosed up against the sandbags. Heat shimmers over everything. Far to the east, artillery grumbles like distant furniture being moved.

SISTER MARGUERITE OLORUNFEMI-DAVIES (30s, white apron gone yellow, sleeves rolled) pushes a gurney between tents. Walking beside her, CAPTAIN JOSIAH TREMBLAY-ASANTE (40s, surgeon, eyes red from lack of sleep) carries a bag of plasma.

At the edge of the tent village, PRIVATE HAKON BJORKLUND-RAMIREZ (20s, rifle, a shaving cut on his jaw) stands watch beside a water barrel. Next to him, SERGEANT PERPETUA ADEYEMI-STONE (30s, sunburnt nose, field cap) scans the horizon with binoculars.

Under a canvas awning, COLONEL BARNABAS WHITLOCK-NKEMELU (60s, gray moustache, a field desk piled with paper) writes with a stub of pencil.

MARGUERITE
Captain, he's bleeding through again.

JOSIAH
I see it.

MARGUERITE
Should I--

JOSIAH
Wait.

They reach a tent flap. Josiah ducks inside. Marguerite parks the gurney and steps back, hands on hips, drawing a long breath.

HAKON
(low)
Sergeant, is that thunder?

PERPETUA
It's the guns.

HAKON
It sounds different.

PERPETUA
It's closer.

HAKON
Closer to who?

PERPETUA
To us, Private.

He glances at the barrel. She keeps scanning. Wind lifts a corner of the tent. A flap slaps.

WHITLOCK
(without looking up)
Sergeant Adeyemi-Stone.

PERPETUA
Sir?

WHITLOCK
What does it look like?

PERPETUA
Dust, sir. A lot of it. A column, maybe. Fifteen miles.

WHITLOCK
Ours or theirs?

PERPETUA
Can't tell yet.

WHITLOCK
Tell me when you can.

She nods. He returns to his paper. She and Hakon exchange a look.

MARGUERITE
(calling over)
Sergeant, we have two ambulances unloading. Do I ready beds?

PERPETUA
Ready the beds.

HAKON
Ready the beds?

PERPETUA
You heard.

Marguerite is already moving. Josiah reappears from the tent, wiping his hands on a cloth.

JOSIAH
How many?

MARGUERITE
Two ambulances, six stretchers.

JOSIAH
All walking?

MARGUERITE
Two are not.

JOSIAH
(muttering)
They never are.

HAKON
Sergeant, the dust is turning.

PERPETUA
Where?

HAKON
Toward us.

Whitlock lays down his pencil. Rises, slow. Shades his eyes.

WHITLOCK
Everyone, listen. Pack the instruments. Load what we can carry. We move at dusk.

JOSIAH
We have fourteen men who cannot move.

WHITLOCK
Then they stay with a medic and a white flag.

MARGUERITE
I'll stay.

JOSIAH
No, I will.

MARGUERITE
Captain--

JOSIAH
You will lead the others, Sister. You know the road.

PERPETUA
Sir, we have a truck.

WHITLOCK
One truck.

PERPETUA
One truck, yes, sir. And a jeep.

WHITLOCK
Then we will take them both.

HAKON
And if the dust is ours?

WHITLOCK
Then we will have wasted an afternoon.

He turns back to his desk, sits, takes up his pencil, and resumes writing. His hand is steady. Marguerite and Josiah look at each other, then at the bleeding man on the gurney.

MARGUERITE
(softly)
We can still do this one.

JOSIAH
We can.

They push the gurney together into the tent. Hakon wipes his forehead. Perpetua lowers the binoculars, wipes the lenses on her sleeve, and raises them again.

PERPETUA
(to herself)
Come on. Show me a flag.

The dust column swells over the ridge, brown against the white sky, and keeps coming.
